      "side": 1,
      "message": "Using a single global variable to store the interrupt GPIO causes a race condition on boards with multiple BMA4xx sensors on different GPIOs (like Veluza).\n\nIf Sensor A and Sensor B interrupt simultaneously:\n1. Both top halves run. `bma4xx_int_signal` ends up storing Sensor B\u0027s pin (`PIN_B`).\n2. The motion task processes Sensor B first. It reads INT_STATUS (clearing the latch, `PIN_B` goes HIGH), processes the FIFO, and checks `irq_pin_asserted()`. It checks `PIN_B`, sees it is HIGH, and safely skips the re-read.\n3. The motion task processes Sensor A. It reads INT_STATUS (clearing the latch, `PIN_A` goes HIGH) and begins reading the FIFO.\n4. If a new interrupt for Sensor A arrives during its FIFO read, `PIN_A` goes LOW again.\n5. Sensor A checks `irq_pin_asserted()`. However, it checks `bma4xx_int_signal` (which is still `PIN_B`).\n6. Since `PIN_B` is HIGH, Sensor A incorrectly assumes its own pin is HIGH and skips the re-read!\n\nSensor A\u0027s pin remains LOW. Since the EC GPIO interrupt is edge-triggered, Sensor A will not fire another interrupt and its data will be stuck until the motion task is woken up again (e.g., by Sensor B\u0027s next interrupt).\n\nTo safely skip the re-read, the driver must check the specific GPIO pin associated with the sensor being processed. If the driver cannot determine its own pin (e.g., by storing it in `drv_data`), it is safer to always re-read the status or disable this optimization for multi-sensor configurations.",
